  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Fundamental Period",
    "lesson_core_move": "Determine the smallest positive time shift $T_0$ that satisfies $x(t+T_0)=x(t) for a periodic continuous-time signal or sinusoid and interpret it as the signal's fundamental period$.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Fundamental Period",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Fundamental Period",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A continuous-time signal is periodic when there is a positive time shift that makes the signal repeat itself for every $t$. The test is whether shifting by $T$ leaves the waveform unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t+T)=x(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The fundamental period, written $T_0$, is the smallest positive shift that works. If several positive shifts repeat the same signal, $T_0$ is the first one you would name.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T_0 = \\min\\{T>0 : x(t+T)=x(t)\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Any larger integer multiple of $T_0$ is also a period, but it is not fundamental. So the question is not just whether the signal repeats, but what the smallest positive repeat shift is.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A simple repeating sinusoid or waveform makes the idea concrete: one visible cycle gives a picture of the smallest shift that returns the signal to the same shape. That is the meaning of $T_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use $T_0$ as the basic repeat time for any periodic signal. Later sections will use it to connect period with angular frequency and harmonic structure.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Fundamental Period",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Checking Whether a Time Shift Is a Period",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Determine whether $T=2$ is a period of $x(t)=\\cos(\\pi t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the proposed shift $T=2$. To test it, substitute $t+2$ into the signal and check whether the result equals $x(t)$ for every $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t+2)=\\cos(\\pi(t+2))=\\cos(\\pi t+2\\pi)=\\cos(\\pi t)=x(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The angle increases by $2\\pi$, and cosine repeats after a full $2\\pi$ turn. So the shifted signal matches the original signal for every $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, $T=2$ is a period of $x(t)=\\cos(\\pi t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Fundamental Period",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding the Smallest Positive Repeating Time Shift",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the fundamental period of $x(t)=\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}t\\right)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the coefficient of $t$ inside the cosine. To find the fundamental period, set the phase advance from $t$ to $t+T_0$ equal to $2\\pi$ and solve for the smallest positive $T_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t+T_0)=\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}(t+T_0)\\right)=\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}t+\\frac{\\pi}{2}T_0\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{\\pi}{2}T_0=2\\pi \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad T_0=4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the smallest positive shift that produces one full $2\\pi$ phase wrap, so the fundamental period is $T_0=4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Fundamental Period",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Distinguishing a Period from the Fundamental Period",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=\\cos(2\\pi t)$, determine whether $T=2$ is the fundamental period or just a period.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the proposed shift $T=2$. First substitute it into the signal and check whether the waveform repeats exactly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t+2)=\\cos(2\\pi(t+2))=\\cos(2\\pi t+4\\pi)=\\cos(2\\pi t)=x(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $T=2$ is a valid period. But to decide whether it is fundamental, find the smallest positive shift that produces the same repeat.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "2\\pi T_0=2\\pi \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad T_0=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the smallest positive repeating shift is $T_0=1$, the shift $T=2$ is only a period. It is a larger multiple of the fundamental period, not the fundamental period itself.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Fundamental Period",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Finding the Fundamental Period from One Complete Cycle",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A periodic signal completes one full cycle from $t=1$ to $t=7$. What is its fundamental period?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the stated start and end of one complete cycle. The fundamental period is the length of that repeating interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T_0=7-1=6",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the signal repeats every $6$ time units. That interval length is the fundamental period.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  "question_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Fundamental Period",
    "lesson_core_move": "Determine the smallest positive time shift $T_0$ that satisfies $x(t+T_0)=x(t) for a periodic continuous-time signal or sinusoid and interpret it as the signal's fundamental period$.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Fundamental Period",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Checking Whether a Time Shift Is a Period",
        "step_type": "example",
        "question_type": "multiple-choice",
        "answer_cardinality": "single",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "question_count": 2,
        "questions": [
          {
            "question_index": 1,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Is $T=1$ a period for $x(t)=\\cos(\\pi t)$?",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "No, because $x(t+1)=\\cos(\\pi(t+1))=\\cos(\\pi t+\\pi)=-\\cos(\\pi t)\\neq x(t)$ for every $t$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Yes, because $x(t+1)=x(t)$ for every $t$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Yes, because a sign flip still counts as the same signal.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "No, because the equality holds only at a few times.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "E",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Yes, because $1$ is half of the fundamental period $2$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              }
            ],
            "correct_choice_labels": [
              "A"
            ],
            "correct_answer": "No, because $x(t+1)=\\cos(\\pi(t+1))=\\cos(\\pi t+\\pi)=-\\cos(\\pi t)\\neq x(t)$ for every $t$.",
            "answer_explanation_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Substitute the proposed shift into the signal and compare the result with the original formula.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              },
              {
                "block_type": "display-math",
                "text": "",
                "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nx(t+1) &= \\cos(\\pi(t+1)) \\\\\n&= \\cos(\\pi t+\\pi) \\\\\n&= -\\cos(\\pi t) \\\\\n&\\neq x(t)\n\\end{aligned}",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              },
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Because the shifted signal is the negative of the original, the equality fails for every $t$. So $T=1$ is not a period.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "question_index": 2,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Is $T=4$ a period for $x(t)=\\cos(\\pi t)$?",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Yes, because $x(t+4)=\\cos(\\pi(t+4))=\\cos(\\pi t+4\\pi)=x(t)$ for every $t$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "No, because $4$ is not the smallest positive period.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Yes, because the signal matches at $t=0$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "No, because $x(t+4)=-x(t)$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "E",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "No, because the signal is not periodic.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              }
            ],
            "correct_choice_labels": [
              "A"
            ],
            "correct_answer": "Yes, because $x(t+4)=\\cos(\\pi(t+4))=\\cos(\\pi t+4\\pi)=x(t)$ for every $t$.",
            "answer_explanation_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Substitute the proposed shift into the signal and compare the result with the original formula.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              },
              {
                "block_type": "display-math",
                "text": "",
                "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nx(t+4) &= \\cos(\\pi(t+4)) \\\\\n&= \\cos(\\pi t+4\\pi) \\\\\n&= \\cos(\\pi t) \\\\\n&= x(t)\n\\end{aligned}",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              },
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "The equality holds for every $t$, so $T=4$ is a valid period. The task is to check one proposed shift, not to find the smallest one.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Fundamental Period",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding the Smallest Positive Repeating Time Shift",
        "step_type": "example",
        "question_type": "multiple-choice",
        "answer_cardinality": "single",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "question_count": 2,
        "questions": [
          {
            "question_index": 1,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Find the fundamental period of $x(t)=\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}t\\right)$.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$6$",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$3$",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$12$",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$\\frac{3}{\\pi}$",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "E",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$\\frac{\\pi}{3}$",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              }
            ],
            "correct_choice_labels": [
              "A"
            ],
            "correct_answer": "$6$",
            "answer_explanation_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "The cue is the coefficient of $t$ inside the sine. For a sinusoid $\\sin(\\omega t)$, the fundamental period is the smallest positive $T_0$ such that $\\omega T_0=2\\pi$.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              },
              {
                "block_type": "display-math",
                "text": "",
                "latex": "\\frac{\\pi}{3}T_0=2\\pi \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad T_0=6",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              },
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "So the fundamental period is $6$.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "question_index": 2,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Find the fundamental period of $x(t)=\\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{5}t+\\frac{\\pi}{7}\\right)$.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$5$",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$2.5$",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$10$",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$2\\pi$",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "E",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$\\frac{2\\pi}{5}$",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              }
            ],
            "correct_choice_labels": [
              "A"
            ],
            "correct_answer": "$5$",
            "answer_explanation_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "The phase shift does not change the period. Only the coefficient of $t$ matters, so use $\\omega=\\frac{2\\pi}{5}$.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              },
              {
                "block_type": "display-math",
                "text": "",
                "latex": "\\frac{2\\pi}{5}T_0=2\\pi \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad T_0=5",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              },
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "So the fundamental period is $5$.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
